{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"All seven children are now CLOSED (S0-S6). This epic still cannot close, and the gap is worth stating precisely because \"every child is closed\" is exactly the signal that invites a premature epic close.\n\nThe children assert CODE. This epic's own acceptance criteria assert the PRODUCTION OUTCOME. Those are different claims, and right now three of the seven are false on the live host.\n\n**AC2 — FALSE.** *\"Dedicated gate-scratch EBS volume exists, IS MOUNTED at a documented path, IS USED by gate snapshots and review clone tempdirs, and has its own disk alarm.\"* The volume exists and the alarm exists; the mount does not. Confirmed directly on the host this morning: `nvme2n1`, 50G, **no fstype**, `file -s` reports `data` — i.e. raw, never formatted. `gate_scratch_mounted` publishes 0 on every sample. This is ticket `dcc3`, and it is the S1 deliverable that never entered service. Until it mounts, the \"is used by\" half is false too — gate scratch is still landing on root, which is the condition this epic was created to end.\n\n**AC3 — FALSE for at least one named accumulator.** *\"Each named root accumulator has an ENFORCED cap and its own CloudWatch alarm at/near cap.\"* The alarms all exist. The enforcement does not, in three places:\n- BuildKit cache measured at **5.875 GB against a 5.00 GiB (5.368 GB) budget** — roughly 9% over, 104 retained entries, 0 active. Not enforced.\n- `/var/tmp` hard quota (S4) and the per-container writable-layer quota (S5) both need XFS `pquota` on the ROOT filesystem, which requires `rootflags=pquota` and a REBOOT. Neither is in force.\n- S5's reaper bounds EXITED containers only and its own report states it cannot reclaim a RUNNING container's writable layer at all — so for the live compose set it is measurement plus an alarm, not a cap.\n\n**AC5 — UNVERIFIED.** *\"`scowlful-bovine-dragonfly` is linked as the carried-over `/var/gerrit` sibling and is resolved or explicitly dispositioned BEFORE EPIC CLOSE.\"* I have not checked its state; whoever closes this must, since the criterion names epic close explicitly.\n\nThe other four (ADR landed, existing alarms not regressed, runbooks, portability) I believe hold on the children's evidence, but I have not re-verified them at epic level and am not ticking anything.\n\n**Why I am flagging this rather than just leaving it open.** Two of these three failures are the same defect class this epic exists to fix, recurring one level up: a deliverable that is built, merged, closed, and NOT IN SERVICE. `dcc3` is S1's; `renowned-corked-hapuku` records the same shape. An epic closed on child-count would convert \"we shipped the code\" into \"the host has a bounded ceiling\", which is precisely the claim that is not true.\n\n**What remains, concretely:** mount the gate-scratch volume (needs a quiet window — mounting hides ~9.3 GB of live data under the mount point); decide on the `rootflags=pquota` reboot, which takes Gerrit, the MCP server and the review-bot down together and is an operator call I am not making; and establish whether the BuildKit overshoot is a misconfiguration or `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace` behaving as the best-effort target it is documented to be. Note the measurement that revealed the overshoot was itself hidden by a clamped metric — `undefiled-revivable-gemsbok`, linked `discovered_from` here.\n\nNot closing. Not ticking. Recording so the next reader does not mistake a complete child list for a delivered outcome."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788603725617937808,"uuid":"d9ebe673-8f33-4e88-b9bb-3296e595fcac"}